{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"People run language models on their own computers because the cloud is convenient, but it is not always the right fit. Privacy is a major reason: prompts, documents, code, medical notes, legal drafts, or business plans may contain information people do not want sent to a third party or stored in someone else’s logs. Keeping inference local reduces that exposure and makes data handling easier to understand.","reasoning_content":"The user wants a short essay in English about why people run language models on their own computers instead of in the cloud. Need produce final answer only. Need ensure essay is short, coherent, maybe 4-6 paragraphs or one cohesive short essay. Need discuss privacy, cost, control, latency, offline/reliability, customization, data sovereignty, security, independence from providers, hardware/software ownership. Avoid overclaiming; mention tradeoffs. Need answer in English. No need for citations. Need be careful not to include hidden thinking. Final should be just essay.\n\nNeed craft concise essay. Maybe title optional. Short essay ~250-350 words. Ensure no markdown excessive? Fine.\n\nLet's draft:\n\n\"People run language models on their own computers because the cloud is convenient but not always the right fit. Privacy is central: prompts and documents may contain personal, medical, legal, financial, or proprietary information; keeping inference local reduces exposure to third-party logs and retention policies. Control matters too: local models can be tuned, fine-tuned, constrained, and audited without waiting for provider approval or changing terms. Cost can also favor ownership for heavy, repeated use, though upfront hardware and maintenance are real. Latency and reliability: local inference can be faster and available offline, useful in remote areas, air-gapped environments, or when network access is unreliable. Independence from vendors: cloud services can change pricing, deprecate models, or restrict use; local deployment gives continuity. There are tradeoffs: local hardware may be slower, less powerful, and harder to update; cloud offers scale and managed infrastructure. Still, for many users, the appeal is a balance of autonomy, privacy, and predictability. The cloud remains attractive for broad access and peak capacity, but running models locally turns AI from a rented service into a tool under one's own control.\"\n\nNeed maybe mention \"own computers\" includes laptops/desktops/servers. Good. Short essay. Final only."}}],"created":1795504250,"model":"/models/unsloth/Inkling-2-Mini-GGUF/Inkling-2-Mini-UD-Q3_K_XL.gguf","system_fingerprint":"b12651-d426a3774","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":512,"prompt_tokens":37,"total_tokens":549,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0}},"id":"chatcmpl-pviRsIVDBR8CqQ6pwqoN9LYny4VDlEAv","timings":{"cache_n":0,"prompt_n":37,"prompt_ms":181.759,"prompt_per_token_ms":4.912405405405405,"prompt_per_second":203.56626081789625,"predicted_n":512,"predicted_ms":9892.465,"predicted_per_token_ms":19.3590313111546,"predicted_per_second":51.65547717378833}}
